  logK25 and dH are the PRINTED values of the sources below (standard state ideal solution at 1 mol/kg, 298.15 K):
      pK1 = 7.184, dH1 = 21.1 kJ/mol, dCp 25 J/mol/K (grade AAA)
  Source: Goldberg, Kishore & Lennen, J. Phys. Chem. Ref. Data 31 (2002) 231; standard state: hypothetical ideal solution of unit molality, 298.15 K, 0.1 MPa (Section 8, p. 359).
  This record carries the one step (the sign flips with the direction written).
  The constant-dCp term of the printed dCp is NOT carried: the record grammar has no place for it (a
  van't Hoff integral with a constant dCp is exactly the `analytic` A1, A3, A4 terms; proposed, not done).
  Goldberg gives no formation data for a buffer.  The species are the acid form(s) and the conjugate base(s) of the section, charges as the section states them, the formula the section's own empirical formula.
\*-----------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
recordType aqueousSpeciation;
schemaVersion 1;

species HMOPS; masters ( { ion MOPS; nu 1; } { ion H; nu 1; } );
      logK25 7.184; dH -21100; source "Goldberg, Kishore & Lennen, J. Phys. Chem. Ref. Data 31 (2002) 231 -- Section 8 summary, p. 360, and Section 7.48, p. 313: pK1 7.184, dH1 21.1 kJ/mol (pK, dH_ion at 298.15 K)";
recordType aqueousSpeciation;
schemaVersion 1;

species MnHCO3; ion "MnHCO3+"; z +1; masters ( { ion MnII; nu 1; } { ion HCO3; nu 1; } );
      logK25 1.95; source "USGS PHREEQC phreeqc.dat (public domain)";

/*--------------------------------*- Choupo -*--------------------------------*\
  chemistry/HLac-formation.dat -- the neutral lactic acid from its anion:
      Lac- + H+  <=>  HLac

  logK25 = +pK = 3.862 and dH = +251 J/mol: Nims & Smith, J. Biol. Chem. 113 (1936) 145,
  Table II, p. 148 (pK 3.862, dH -60 cal/mol at 25 C), the sign flipped for the direction
  written.  The inverse of Lac-formation.dat (see there): a case declares one master and
  the closure activates the record reachable from it.
\*-----------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
recordType aqueousSpeciation;
schemaVersion 1;

species HLac; masters ( { ion Lac; nu 1; } { ion H; nu 1; } );
      logK25 3.862; dH 251; source "Nims & Smith, J. Biol. Chem. 113 (1936) 145, Table II p. 148 and eq. (2) p. 149: pK 3.862 at 25 C; dH -60 cal/mol = -251 J/mol, sign flipped";
/*--------------------------------*- Choupo -*--------------------------------*\
  chemistry/Cac-formation.dat -- a dissociation of cacodylic acid (dimethylarsinic acid), written from the master:
      HCac  <=>  Cac + H+
  Made by bin/curate/species_c373.py from data/curation/batches/species_c373/*.tsv.

  logK25 and dH are the PRINTED values of the sources below (standard state ideal solution at 1 mol/kg, 298.15 K):
      pK1 = 1.78, dH1 = -3.5 kJ/mol (grade CBB)
      pK2 = 6.28, dH2 = -3.0 kJ/mol, dCp -86 J/mol/K (grade BBB)
  Source: Goldberg, Kishore & Lennen, J. Phys. Chem. Ref. Data 31 (2002) 231; standard state: hypothetical ideal solution of unit molality, 298.15 K, 0.1 MPa (Section 8, p. 359).
  The master is HCac, the form a case declares; this record carries step 2 (the sign flips with the direction written).
  The constant-dCp term of the printed dCp is NOT carried: the record grammar has no place for it (a
  van't Hoff integral with a constant dCp is exactly the `analytic` A1, A3, A4 terms; proposed, not done).
  The section header gives the empirical formula C2H7AsO2 (molecular weight 138.00); the line "where HL=C2H6AsO2" of the same section and of Section 8 is a misprint (it is the anion's formula).  The header formula is used.
\*-----------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
recordType aqueousSpeciation;
schemaVersion 1;

species Cac; masters ( { ion HCac; nu 1; } { ion H; nu -1; } );
      logK25 -6.28; dH -3000; source "Goldberg, Kishore & Lennen, J. Phys. Chem. Ref. Data 31 (2002) 231 -- Section 8 summary, p. 359, and Section 7.17, p. 261: pK2 6.28, dH2 -3.0 kJ/mol (pK, dH_ion at 298.15 K)";
/*--------------------------------*- Choupo -*--------------------------------*\
  chemistry/HBenz-formation.dat -- the benzoic acid master as the sum of its fully dissociated form (the inverse of the records written from the master; a case declares one master and the closure activates the record reachable from it):
      Benz(-1) + 1 H+  <=>  HBenz
  Made by bin/curate/species_c373.py from data/curation/batches/species_c373/*.tsv.

  logK25 and dH are the PRINTED values of the sources below (standard state ideal solution at 1 mol/kg, 298.15 K):
      pK1 = 4.20, dH1 = 0.335 kJ/mol
  Source: Shock, Organic acids in hydrothermal solutions: standard molal thermodynamic properties of carboxylic acids, and estimates of dissociation constants at high temperatures and pressures, Am. J. Sci. 295 (1995) 496-580; H+ = 0, 25 C, 1 bar, hypothetical 1 molal ideal solution (Appendix 1); cal/mol and cal/mol/K converted at 4.184 exactly; a superscript on a table value names how the author obtained it (Table 4 footnotes, p. 519; Table 10 footnotes, p. 544).
  This record carries the one step (the sign flips with the direction written).
  The constant-dCp term of the printed dCp is NOT carried: the record grammar has no place for it (a
  van't Hoff integral with a constant dCp is exactly the `analytic` A1, A3, A4 terms; proposed, not done).
  The neutral master carries its own aqueous formation data (no component + solution pair is in the catalogue for this acid).  Source: Shock (1995); values the paper marks as estimated carry `origin estimated` per value.
\*-----------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
recordType aqueousSpeciation;
schemaVersion 1;

species HBenz; masters ( { ion Benz; nu 1; } { ion H; nu 1; } );
      logK25 4.2; dH -335; source "Shock, Organic acids in hydrothermal solutions: standard molal thermodynamic properties of carboxylic acids, and estimates of dissociation constants at high temperatures and pressures, Am. J. Sci. 295 (1995) 496-580 -- pK: Table 7, p. 534 (log K at 25 C, as printed), dH: the printed DfH of the anion minus that of the acid in Table 4, p. 518 (PDF p. 23): pK1 4.20, dH1 0.335 kJ/mol (read from a page image) (pK, dH_ion at 298.15 K)";
recordType aqueousSpeciation;
schemaVersion 1;

species MnCl3; ion "MnCl3-"; z -1; masters ( { ion MnII; nu 1; } { ion Cl; nu 3; } );
      logK25 -0.31; source "USGS PHREEQC phreeqc.dat (public domain)";

recordType aqueousSpeciation;
schemaVersion 1;

species MgSO4aq; ion "MgSO4"; z 0; masters ( { ion Mg; nu 1; } { ion SO4; nu 1; } );
      logK25 2.42; dH 19000; analytic ( 0 0.00964 -136 ); validC ( 0 200 ); source "USGS PHREEQC phreeqc.dat (public domain); lit: epsomite/hexahydrite/kieserite solubilities, 0 - 200 oC";

/*--------------------------------*- Choupo -*--------------------------------*\
  conventions/Sander-Hxp-v1.dat -- a VERSIONED, IMMUTABLE convention profile
  (equilibrium-parameterisation identity ADR, ratified 2026-07-26).

  The convention of Sander's Henry compilation as adopted by the
  parameters/Henry/ pair records: pressure/mole-fraction Henry constant,
  p_i = H_xp * x_i, ideal-gas standard state, van't Hoff temperature form.
  A record declares `convention Sander-Hxp-v1;` and overrides only what
  deviates; the resolver can print the fully expanded form.  Once
  published this profile NEVER changes -- corrections mint a -v2.
\*-----------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
recordType    conventionProfile;
schemaVersion 1;

name                 Sander-Hxp-v1;
gasStandardState     idealGas;
compositionBasis     liquidMoleFraction;
referencePressure    101325 Pa;
temperatureForm      vantHoff;
solventReference     pureLiquid;
source               "Sander, R. (2015) Atmos. Chem. Phys. 15, 4399 (H_xp form)";
/*--------------------------------*- Choupo -*--------------------------------*\
  conventions/Edwards-Hkgatm-v1.dat -- a VERSIONED, IMMUTABLE convention
  profile (equilibrium-parameterisation identity ADR, ratified 2026-07-26).

  The convention of Edwards, Maurer, Newman & Prausnitz, AIChE J. 24(6):
  966-976 (1978) for the Henry constants of volatile weak electrolytes in
  water -- Table 3 / Eq 13.  It is NOT the Sander-Hxp-v1 convention and the
  two must never be mixed in one calculation:

    * the composition basis is MOLALITY, not liquid mole fraction, so
      Edwards' H has units of kg-atm/mol.  Its numerical value differs from
      an H_xp by the factor 1/M_w (about 55.5) plus the activity
      convention;
    * the reference pressure is the SATURATION PRESSURE OF WATER at the
      system temperature, P_w^s -- not 1 atm.  H is tabulated at P_w^s and
      carried to the system pressure by the Krichevsky-Kasarnovsky term of
      Eq 11,  ln H^(P) = ln H^(P_w^s) + vbar_a^inf (P - P_w^s)/(R T),
      with the infinite-dilution partial molar volumes of Table 2;
    * the temperature form is Eq 13, ln H = B1/T + B2 ln T + B3 T + B4,
      with T in KELVIN (the paper quotes ranges of validity in degC but
      writes every correlation in K);
    * the solute activity coefficient is the UNSYMMETRIC gamma* of Eq 8
      (Pitzer truncation, Edwards parameterisation), normalised to unity at
      infinite dilution -- so an H from this profile is only meaningful
      alongside `activityModel edwardsPitzer`.

  THIS PROFILE DOES NOT REPLACE Sander-Hxp-v1 FOR THE SAME PAIR.  The D2
  identity contract makes a parameterisation the unit of identity: NH3-water
  under Sander and NH3-water under Edwards are two curated records that
  coexist, each naming its own convention, and a case selects one.  Neither
  is "the" Henry constant for ammonia.

  Once published this profile NEVER changes -- corrections mint a -v2.
\*-----------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
recordType    conventionProfile;
schemaVersion 1;

name                 Edwards-Hkgatm-v1;
gasStandardState     idealGas;
compositionBasis     molality;
referencePressure    waterSaturation;
pressureCorrection   KrichevskyKasarnovsky;
temperatureForm      EdwardsEq13;
soluteReference      infiniteDilutionUnsymmetric;
solventReference     pureLiquid;
requiredActivityModel edwardsPitzer;
source               "Edwards, Maurer, Newman & Prausnitz, AIChE J. 24(6):966-976 (1978), Table 3 / Eqs 11 and 13";
/*--------------------------------*- Choupo -*--------------------------------*\
  conventions/PHREEQC-gasMolal-v1.dat -- a VERSIONED, IMMUTABLE convention
  profile (equilibrium-parameterisation identity ADR, ratified 2026-07-26).

  The operational convention of the aqueous speciation network's gas-liquid
  records (PHREEQC PHASES): molality-scale activity of the dissolved
  species, a_i = K(T) * p_i[atm], water-referenced infinite-dilution
  standard state, anchored-analytic/van't Hoff temperature forms.
\*-----------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
recordType    conventionProfile;
schemaVersion 1;

name                 PHREEQC-gasMolal-v1;
gasStandardState     idealGas;
compositionBasis     molality;
referencePressure    101325 Pa;
temperatureForm      anchoredAnalyticOrVantHoff;
solventReference     aqueousInfiniteDilution;
source               "USGS PHREEQC PHASES convention (public domain)";
/*--------------------------------*- Choupo -*--------------------------------*\
  conventions/MilleroConventional-v1.dat -- a VERSIONED, IMMUTABLE convention
  profile (the D2 identity pattern; once published it NEVER changes --
  corrections mint a -v2).

  The single-ion standard-state (infinite-dilution) partial molar volume
  convention of Millero's critical compilation: V0(H+) == 0 exactly, at
  298.15 K and 1 atm, water reference.  Values on this profile are
  CONVENTIONAL, not physical -- a convention shift adds lambda*z_i to every
  ion's V0, so ONLY ELECTRONEUTRAL SUMS are observable (the shift cancels
  exactly when Sum n_i z_i = 0, which is why the aqueousAnalysis consumer
  charge-closes its inventory BEFORE summing).  Negative values (Na+, Mg2+,
  Ca2+, OH-) are artifacts of the H+ = 0 anchor plus real electrostriction;
  they are not "negative volumes of matter".

  CAUTIONARY PRECEDENT, recorded where the numbers live: an uncited
  secondary electrolyte table in circulation carries this same set, with K+
  SIGN-FLIPPED (-9 against the primary's +9.02 cm3/mol) --
  six neighbouring values matching the primary to the printed digit.  An
  uncited value cannot be audited; every V0 on this profile cites the
  primary, and the check_derived_density gate anchors electroneutral sums
  against measured solution densities so a flipped sign cannot ship.
\*-----------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
recordType    conventionProfile;
schemaVersion 1;

name                 MilleroConventional-v1;
property             standardStatePartialMolarVolume;
ionAnchor            "V0(H+) = 0 exactly";
temperatureReference 298.15 K;
pressureReference    101325 Pa;
solventReference     water;
observableRule       "electroneutral sums only; single-ion values are conventional";
source               "Millero, F. J. (1971) Chem. Rev. 71, 147 -- partial molal volumes of electrolytes in aqueous solution (conventional scale)";
reviewStatus         interim;    // transcribed values staged for batch primary review (Vitor)
/*--------------------------------*- Choupo -*-----------------------*\
  THE TREE OF CHEMISTRY -- the graph's substances, roots and sources.
  This record is the HEADER of ONE directed graph whose edges live in the
  sibling `recordType productionRoutes` files of this folder.  Nodes are
  substances; an edge is a named route (a reaction, a separation or a
  lumped process step) from its reactants to its target.  Nothing in the
  engine reads this folder (README.md beside it); its consumer is the
  EduTool `The tree of chemistry` (DEV.md 4c C191 A), and its gate is
  bin/curate/check_chemistry_graph.py, which re-reads every file here,
  balances every reaction with the engine's own formula grammar and
  atomic-weight table, and holds every node and edge to the rules the
  README states.
  THE MESSAGE (the architect's, DEV.md 4c C191): the hundred-odd commodity
  products grow from SEVEN organic roots and FOUR inorganic ones, and the
  sustainable transition replaces the ROOTS, not the leaves.  A node's
  role (feedstock, root, intermediate, product) is NOT stored: a root is
  declared below, and the rest is read off the topology (no inbound edge =
  feedstock; no outbound edge = product), so that it can never disagree
  with the edges.  Likewise a node that has a catalogue component record
  carries neither formula nor CAS here: both are READ from
  data/standards/components/<name>.dat (one home), and the gate holds the
  graph to the catalogue.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/


recordType    routeGraph;
schemaVersion 1;
name          treeOfChemistry;
description   "The commodity chemicals as one directed graph: feedstocks, seven organic and four inorganic roots, intermediates and products, joined by named production routes with their stoichiometry, yield, co-products and citation.";

//  THE ROOTS.  Seven organic, four inorganic (the architect's list).  A root
//  with several members is ONE root (the C4s, the xylenes, chlorine WITH caustic
//  -- co-produced -- and synthesis gas as the CO + H2 pair with methanol as its
//  first derivative).  `group` is organic | inorganic and the gate counts 7 + 4.
roots
(
    { name ethylene;                group organic;   members ( ethylene ); label "ethylene"; }
    { name propylene;               group organic;   members ( propylene ); label "propylene"; }
    { name C4s;                     group organic;   members ( 13Butadiene 1Butene isoButene ); label "the C4s: butadiene and the butenes"; }
    { name benzene;                 group organic;   members ( benzene ); label "benzene"; }
    { name toluene;                 group organic;   members ( toluene ); label "toluene"; }
    { name xylenes;                 group organic;   members ( pXylene oXylene mXylene ); label "the xylenes"; }
    { name syngas;                  group organic;   members ( CO H2 methanol ); label "synthesis gas (CO + H2), with methanol as its first derivative"; }
    { name ammonia;                 group inorganic; members ( NH3 ); label "ammonia"; }
    { name sulfuricAcid;            group inorganic; members ( H2SO4 ); label "sulfuric acid"; }
    { name chlorAlkali;             group inorganic; members ( Cl2 NaOH ); label "chlorine and caustic soda, co-produced"; }
    { name nitricAndPhosphoricAcid; group inorganic; members ( HNO3 H3PO4 ); label "the nitric and phosphoric acids"; }
);

//  THE PROCESS FAMILIES an edge may declare (`family`), each with its meaning.
//  One home: the gate reads this list, not a copy of it.
processFamilies
(
    { name separation;            meaning "a physical separation of a cut or a mixture: distillation, extraction, crystallisation, adsorption"; }
    { name steamCracking;         meaning "thermal pyrolysis of a hydrocarbon feed in the presence of steam, 800-900 C, milliseconds"; }
    { name catalyticCracking;     meaning "fluid catalytic cracking of a heavy cut over a zeolite, 500-550 C"; }
    { name catalyticReforming;    meaning "naphtha dehydrocyclisation/isomerisation over Pt on alumina to aromatics + hydrogen"; }
    { name reforming;             meaning "steam or autothermal reforming of a light hydrocarbon to CO + H2"; }
    { name gasification;          meaning "partial oxidation of a solid or heavy feed with steam/oxygen to CO + H2"; }
    { name shift;                 meaning "the water-gas shift and its reverse"; }
    { name electrolysis;          meaning "an electrochemical conversion driven by electricity"; }
    { name hydrogenation;         meaning "addition of H2 over a metal catalyst"; }
    { name dehydrogenation;       meaning "removal of H2, thermal or catalytic"; }
    { name dehydration;           meaning "removal of water over an acid catalyst"; }
    { name hydration;             meaning "addition of water over an acid catalyst"; }
    { name oxidation;             meaning "selective oxidation with O2/air over a solid catalyst"; }
    { name ammoxidation;          meaning "oxidation in the presence of NH3 to a nitrile"; }
    { name hydroformylation;      meaning "olefin + CO + H2 to an aldehyde over a Co or Rh complex (oxo)"; }
    { name carbonylation;         meaning "insertion of CO, homogeneously catalysed"; }
    { name alkylation;            meaning "aromatic + olefin over an acid catalyst"; }
    { name isomerisation;         meaning "skeletal or positional rearrangement over an acid or bifunctional catalyst"; }
    { name disproportionation;    meaning "transalkylation of methyl groups between aromatic rings"; }
    { name hydrodealkylation;     meaning "removal of an alkyl group from an aromatic ring with H2"; }
    { name chlorination;          meaning "addition or substitution of chlorine; oxychlorination uses HCl + O2"; }
    { name nitration;             meaning "substitution with HNO3 in mixed acid"; }
    { name esterification;        meaning "acid + alcohol to an ester + water"; }
    { name etherification;        meaning "olefin + alcohol to an ether over an acid resin"; }
    { name polymerisation;        meaning "chain or step growth of a monomer to its polymer"; }
    { name condensation;          meaning "a coupling that releases a small molecule (water, NH3, HCl)"; }
    { name acidBase;              meaning "neutralisation or acid attack with no redox"; }
    { name rearrangement;         meaning "an intramolecular rearrangement (Beckmann, Hock)"; }
    { name synthesis;             meaning "a direct catalytic synthesis from its elements or from syngas"; }
    { name fermentation;          meaning "a microbial conversion"; }
    { name pyrolysis;             meaning "thermal depolymerisation or cracking of a solid/polymer feed"; }
    { name methanation;           meaning "hydrogenation of CO/CO2 to methane"; }
    { name methanolToHydrocarbons; meaning "zeolite-catalysed conversion of methanol to olefins or aromatics"; }
);

//  THE SOURCES every edge cites by id.  `licence` is the grant the SOURCE
//  carries: ccBy4 / ccBy3 (text and figures reusable with attribution),
//  citedWork (a copyrighted work named as the reference for a FACT -- a title
//  or a DOI is a citation, a copied table is not).  `verification` says how
//  well the citation itself is known: checkedCopy (the document was opened and
//  read), bibliographicRecord (title/authors/journal/year corroborated against
//  the Crossref record of the DOI on the date noted; the paper NOT opened),
//  titleOnly (a textbook or encyclopaedia named by title as the standard
//  reference for the route; no page claimed).  No source here is a
//  NonCommercial or no-grant compilation: industry-association statistics
//  (ACC, Cefic, Plastics Europe) were checked on 2026-11-01 and are NOT used,
//  because their terms permit non-commercial use only or state no grant.
sources
(
    {
        name          iea2018;
        citation      "IEA (2018), The Future of Petrochemicals: Towards more sustainable plastics and fertilisers, OECD/IEA, Paris";
        url           "https://www.iea.org/reports/the-future-of-petrochemicals";
        licence       ccBy4;
        licenceNote   "the report page states 'Licence: CC BY 4.0' (read 2026-11-01); the 2018 PDF's own front matter still says 'subject to specific restrictions', the IEA having relicensed its reports since -- the page is the current grant";
        verification  checkedCopy;
        verificationNote "PDF read 2026-11-01; page numbers below are the report's own";
    }
    {
        name          ieaAmmonia2021;
        citation      "IEA (2021), Ammonia Technology Roadmap: Towards more sustainable nitrogen fertiliser production, IEA, Paris";
        url           "https://www.iea.org/reports/ammonia-technology-roadmap";
        licence       ccBy4;
        verification  checkedCopy;
        verificationNote "the report's executive-summary page read 2026-11-01; the full PDF not opened";
    }
    {
        name          ieaHydrogen2019;
        citation      "IEA (2019), The Future of Hydrogen: Seizing today's opportunities, IEA, Paris";
        url           "https://www.iea.org/reports/the-future-of-hydrogen";
        licence       ccBy4;
        verification  checkedCopy;
        verificationNote "report page read 2026-11-01";
    }
    {
        name          weissermelArpe2003;
        citation      "K. Weissermel, H.-J. Arpe, Industrial Organic Chemistry, 4th ed., Wiley-VCH, Weinheim (2003)";
        licence       citedWork;
        verification  titleOnly;
        verificationNote "named as the standard reference for the route; no table or page copied; the stoichiometry is checked by the gate, not quoted from the book";
    }
    {
        name          chauvelLefebvre1989;
        citation      "A. Chauvel, G. Lefebvre, Petrochemical Processes, vols. 1-2, Editions Technip, Paris (1989)";
        licence       citedWork;
        verification  titleOnly;
    }
    {
        name          ullmanns;
        citation      "Ullmann's Encyclopedia of Industrial Chemistry, Wiley-VCH, Weinheim (online edition); the article named in each edge's locus";
        licence       citedWork;
        verification  titleOnly;
    }
    {
        name          kirkOthmer;
        citation      "Kirk-Othmer Encyclopedia of Chemical Technology, Wiley (online edition); the article named in each edge's locus";
        licence       citedWork;
        verification  titleOnly;
    }
    {
        name          moulijn2013;
        citation      "J. A. Moulijn, M. Makkee, A. van Diepen, Chemical Process Technology, 2nd ed., Wiley (2013)";
        licence       citedWork;
        verification  titleOnly;
    }
    {
        name          obrien2005;
        citation      "T. F. O'Brien, T. V. Bommaraju, F. Hine, Handbook of Chlor-Alkali Technology, Springer (2005)";
        licence       citedWork;
        verification  titleOnly;
    }
    {
