/*--------------------------------*- Choupo -*--------------------------------*\
  Component: Glycine (aminoacetic acid), crystal (C2H5NO2).  MW 75.067 g/mol (the engine's atomic weights).

  A NON-VOLATILE crystalline organic solid: never enters the vapour (K = 0), no Antoine block.  Built on the
  citric-acid pattern (components/citricAcid.dat) from the formation data of Miller & Smith-Magowan (1990), C373 phase 3.

  WHAT THIS RECORD CARRIES, AND WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT.

    carries   the crystal formation enthalpy dHf = -528.52 +/- 0.50 kJ/mol
              (Miller & Smith-Magowan, J. Phys. Chem. Ref. Data 19 (1990) 1049, Table 3, p. 1054, row dfH(cr) (their ref. a: the evaluation of Cox & Pilcher 1970 (the footnote lists -528.61 +/- 0.21 kJ/mol from ref. 77NGA/SAB as a later value); read from a page image));
              the heat of solution of the pair file parameters/solution/glycine-water.dat.
    does NOT  a density, a standard entropy s_298 and any heat capacity as record values: no density is
              in a work read for this record, and Miller prints the crystal entropy only as an intermediate of
              its third-law route to the aqueous species (Table 3), which enter through the species records.  An
              energy balance that reaches the crystal's or the solution's SENSIBLE heat therefore refuses by
              the name of what is missing (CLAUDE.md rule 5).

    ALSO BUILT, in their own homes: the glycine ladder (H2Gly, HGly, Gly; chemistry/*-formation), whose zwitterion carries
              the aqueous enthalpy and entropy of Miller's Table 3.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

name        glycine;
formula     C2H5NO2;
MW          75.067;            // kg/kmol

role        nonvolatile;               // crystalline amino acid; never to the vapour (K = 0)
dissociation 1;                 // molecular (the ions are species/, see the header)

standardThermochemistry
{
    dHf_298   -528520;        // J/mol  crystalline [-528.52 +/- 0.50 kJ/mol, Miller & Smith-Magowan 1990, Table 3]
    referenceState     pureSolid;
}

provenance
{
    dHf_298
    {
        origin        literature;
        method        "evaluated enthalpy of formation of the crystal (-528.52 +/- 0.50 kJ/mol)";
        citation      "Miller & Smith-Magowan, J. Phys. Chem. Ref. Data 19 (1990) 1049, Table 3, p. 1054, row dfH(cr) (their ref. a: the evaluation of Cox & Pilcher 1970 (the footnote lists -528.61 +/- 0.21 kJ/mol from ref. 77NGA/SAB as a later value); read from a page image)";
    }
}
