    { name rNH;         MW 15.015; nA 2; dTc 0.013; dPc 0.0114; dVc 29; dTb 52.82; dHf 31.65; dGf 75.61; cpa 11.8; cpb -0.023; cpc 0.000107; cpd -6.28e-08; dHv 6.93; }   // ring >NH
    { name N;           MW 14.007; nA 1; dTc 0.0169; dPc 0.0074; dVc 9; dTb 11.74; dHf 123.34; dGf 163.16; cpa -31.1; cpb 0.227; cpc -0.00032; cpd 1.46e-07; dHv 1.896; }   // tertiary amine
    { name eN;          MW 14.007; nA 1; dTc 0.0255; dPc -0.0099; dVc 0; dTb 74.6; dHf 23.61; dGf 0; cpa 0; cpb 0; cpc 0; cpd 0; dHv 3.335; }   // =N- nonring (thermo lacks Vc/Cp/dGf -> 0; Gf/Cp unreliable for this group)
    { name reN;         MW 14.007; nA 1; dTc 0.0085; dPc 0.0076; dVc 34; dTb 57.55; dHf 55.52; dGf 79.93; cpa 8.83; cpb -0.00384; cpc 4.35e-05; cpd -2.6e-08; dHv 6.528; }   // ring =N-
    { name CN;          MW 26.018; nA 2; dTc 0.0496; dPc -0.0101; dVc 91; dTb 125.66; dHf 88.43; dGf 89.22; cpa 36.5; cpb -0.0733; cpc 0.000184; cpd -1.03e-07; dHv 12.851; }   // nitrile
    { name NO2;         MW 46.005; nA 3; dTc 0.0437; dPc 0.0064; dVc 91; dTb 152.54; dHf -66.57; dGf -16.83; cpa 25.9; cpb -0.00374; cpc 0.000129; cpd -8.88e-08; dHv 16.738; }   // nitro
    { name SH;          MW 33.068; nA 2; dTc 0.0031; dPc 0.0084; dVc 63; dTb 63.56; dHf -17.33; dGf -22.99; cpa 35.3; cpb -0.0758; cpc 0.000185; cpd -1.03e-07; dHv 6.884; }   // thiol
    { name S;           MW 32.060; nA 1; dTc 0.0119; dPc 0.0049; dVc 54; dTb 68.78; dHf 41.87; dGf 33.12; cpa 19.6; cpb -0.00561; cpc 4.02e-05; cpd -2.76e-08; dHv 6.817; }   // sulfide -S-
    { name rS;          MW 32.060; nA 1; dTc 0.0019; dPc 0.0051; dVc 38; dTb 52.1; dHf 39.1; dGf 27.76; cpa 16.7; cpb 0.00481; cpc 2.77e-05; cpd -2.11e-08; dHv 5.984; }   // ring -S-
);
/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  Van Krevelen polymer group contribution -- SLICE 1: DENSITY groups.

  Each group carries two numbers:
    MW  [g/mol]     -- the group's molar mass (atomic masses; a trivial fact).
    Vw  [cm^3/mol]  -- the BONDI van der Waals group volume.

  PROVENANCE OF Vw (the licence-clean path -- read this):
    The Vw values are Bondi, J. Phys. Chem. 68 (1964) 441 -- the PRIMARY source
    per value.  They are NOT copied from the Van Krevelen book table (copyright);
    they are DERIVED from the UNIFAC R_k volume parameters Choupo already ships
    in data/standards/unifac/groups.dat (ChemSep Artistic-2.0, Hansen 1991, whose
    R_k are themselves Bondi van der Waals volumes), via the standard
    Abrams-Prausnitz normalisation:

        Vw = R_k * 15.17  cm^3/mol          (R_k = Vw / 15.17)

    e.g. CH2: R_k = 0.6744 -> Vw = 10.231 cm^3/mol (Bondi's tabulated CH2).
    So every Vw here is reproducible from an already-in-repo, openly-licensed
    number -- no book table was read, no value was hand-typed from VK.

  The PACKING FACTOR k (used as V = k*Vw, rho = M0/V) is NOT a group property --
  it lives in the case op's `polymer { packing k; }` (default 1.60 amorphous;
  ~1.43 crystalline).  It is announced on the console, never hidden.

  DEFERRED (not here -- Slice 2/3): Tg (needs the Yang 2020 modified scheme,
  separately attributed -- NOT bare Van Krevelen), solubility parameter delta,
  Tm, Cp.  Those open parameters are a different, correctly-cited thing.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

groups
(
    // name      MW [g/mol]   Vw [cm3/mol]   (Vw = R_UNIFAC * 15.17; primary Bondi 1964)
    { name CH3;    MW 15.035;   Vw 13.670;
      provenance { origin "Bondi, J. Phys. Chem. 68 (1964) 441"; method "Vw = R_UNIFAC(0.9011) x 15.17"; adoptedVia "data/standards/unifac (Hansen 1991, ChemSep Artistic-2.0)"; } }
    { name CH2;    MW 14.027;   Vw 10.231;
      provenance { origin "Bondi 1964"; method "Vw = R_UNIFAC(0.6744) x 15.17"; adoptedVia "data/standards/unifac"; } }
    { name CH;     MW 13.019;   Vw  6.780;
      provenance { origin "Bondi 1964"; method "Vw = R_UNIFAC(0.4469) x 15.17"; adoptedVia "data/standards/unifac"; } }
    { name C;      MW 12.011;   Vw  3.330;
      provenance { origin "Bondi 1964"; method "Vw = R_UNIFAC(0.2195) x 15.17"; adoptedVia "data/standards/unifac"; } }
    { name ACH;    MW 13.019;   Vw  8.060;
      provenance { origin "Bondi 1964"; method "Vw = R_UNIFAC(0.5313) x 15.17 (aromatic =CH-)"; adoptedVia "data/standards/unifac"; } }
    { name AC;     MW 12.011;   Vw  5.540;
      provenance { origin "Bondi 1964"; method "Vw = R_UNIFAC(0.3652) x 15.17 (aromatic =C<, substituted)"; adoptedVia "data/standards/unifac"; } }
    { name CHCl;   MW 48.472;   Vw 18.781;
      provenance { origin "Bondi 1964"; method "Vw = R_UNIFAC(1.2380) x 15.17 (combined >CHCl)"; adoptedVia "data/standards/unifac"; } }
    { name CH2Cl;  MW 49.480;   Vw 22.230;
      provenance { origin "Bondi 1964"; method "Vw = R_UNIFAC(1.4654) x 15.17 (combined -CH2Cl)"; adoptedVia "data/standards/unifac"; } }
);
# Binary interaction parameters --- naming and format

## Folder layout

One subfolder per activity-coefficient model:

```
parameters/
├── NRTL/        (`ls` is the authority -- a count here went stale once)
├── UNIQUAC/     (`ls` is the authority)
└── ...          (Henry/ Pitzer/ eNRTL/ SRK/ UNIFAC/ EdwardsPitzer/
                  adsorption/ solution/ -- `ls` is the authority)
```

Two corrections landed here on 2026-08-11, both of which had been telling a
reader the opposite of the truth:

* the tree above still showed the pre-Migration-2 `binaryPairs/` layout, and a
  `Wilson/` folder that does not exist;
* the UNIQUAC set was described as "canonical DECHEMA binaries, imported from
  ChemSep".  **All three shipped UNIQUAC files cite Pöpken, Götze & Gmehling,
  Ind. Eng. Chem. Res. 39 (2000) 2601, Table 3** — a primary journal table.
  The bulk ChemSep import was moved to the private `data/local/` tier in the
  legal scrub and is not what ships.  A stale provenance claim that makes
  CLEAN data look encumbered is as much a defect as the reverse, and it is
  harder to notice because nobody audits in that direction.

A pair converted from a published table keeps the raw cal/mol `source{}`
block alongside the converted `parameters{}` (see its `provenance`).  A pair
Choupo REGRESSED to published measurements (C16, 2026-09-28: `origin
fitted;`) has no raw block -- its source is the measurements, named per
article under `provenance.evidence`, and the case that regressed it is named
by `fittedInCase`; `bin/curate/check_regressed_pairs.py` re-runs that case and
holds the record to it.  How such a pair is made, and why a ChemSep or
`thermo`-package table may never be one:
[`docs/design/binary-pairs-from-open-measurements.md`](../../../docs/design/binary-pairs-from-open-measurements.md).  The model name in the folder
must match the dict `model` keyword inside each file.

## Naming convention

Each file holds the interaction parameters for one component pair:

```
<c1>-<c2>.dat
```

where `c1` and `c2` are component names **in alphabetical order** (so
`ethanol-water`, not `water-ethanol`).  The simulator normalises the
order when looking up a file.

Variants of the same pair carry a tag suffix:

```
<c1>-<c2>.dat                       (canonical / active)
<c1>-<c2>.<tag>.dat                  (variant, ignored unless explicitly referenced)
```

**The unsuffixed file is the canonical set the simulator loads.** Tagged
files are dormant alternatives kept for history or comparison.

Typical tags:

| Tag | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `PPO5`           | Poling, Prausnitz, O'Connell 5th ed. |
| `dechema-vol-1`  | DECHEMA VLE Data Collection Vol I |
| `myLab-2026-05`  | own laboratory fit, dated |
| `vendor-default` | a vendor's default parameters (for comparison) |

Tags are free-form lowercase-hyphenated; the **provenance block inside
the file is the truth**, the tag is only a filename hint.

## File format

Every file is an Choupo dict with three blocks:

```
components  ( <c1>  <c2> );        // alphabetical
model       NRTL;                   // matches enclosing folder

parameters
{
    // model-specific entries (e.g., a_ij, b_ij, a_ji, b_ji, alpha for NRTL)
}

provenance
{
    origin        literature | regressed | predictive | estimated | assumed | placeholder;
    source        "<optional raw source label / dataset id>";   // TEXT, never a class
    citation      "<primary reference>";
    method        "<how the values were produced>";             // e.g. "Levenberg-Marquardt"
    methodVersion "<stable method/table id>";                   // for deterministic estimates
    fitData       "<path to CSV>";          // if regressed; else "<not refit>"
    fitDate       "YYYY-MM-DD" | "<literature, ca. YYYY>";
    chi2          <number> | "<not reported>";
    nDataPoints   <number>;
    confidenceIntervals { <param> <stderr>; … }   // optional
    validity { temperature { min <T> K; max <T> K; } note "..."; }
    promotedDespite { identifiable 0; reason "..."; by "..."; date "YYYY-MM-DD"; }  // ONLY on an overridden promotion
    author        "<who curated or fitted>";
    notes         "<free text>";
}
```

**`origin` is the TYPED provenance class** — ranking, resolution priority and
every badge/policy read it.  The canonical classes:

* **`literature`** — measured values from a cited primary source.
* **`regressed`** — fitted to experimental data inside Choupo's fit workflow
  (`fitData`/`chi2`/`confidenceIntervals` populated).
* **`predictive`** — a MODEL's prediction transcribed as a value (a
  UNIFAC-derived surrogate).  Announced on every run that consumes it.
* **`estimated`** — group contribution from the substance's own structure.
* **`assumed`** — an engineering assumption the curator owns (e.g. null
  coefficients = ideality).
* **`placeholder`** — a stub awaiting real values; loudly flagged.

`source` and `citation` are TEXT — a source label and the primary reference —
never a second origin class.  A curated (standards) pair file MUST declare
`origin`; the engine refuses it otherwise.  `promotedDespite` is the auditable
record of a human promoting past a diagnostic: all four fields are required,
and every run that consumes such a pair repeats the warning.

This metadata is the difference between this database and an opaque
proprietary one. Every consumer can audit every number.

## Adding a new pair

For a new component pair under NRTL:

```bash
# 1. Verify the file does not already exist (alphabetical order)
ls data/standards/binaryPairs/NRTL/ | grep -i benzene
ls data/standards/binaryPairs/NRTL/ | grep -i methanol

# 2. Make the directory writable (it is read-only by default)
chmod +w data/standards/binaryPairs/NRTL/

# 3. Create the file (use an existing one as template)
cp data/standards/binaryPairs/NRTL/ethanol-water.dat \
   data/standards/binaryPairs/NRTL/benzene-methanol.dat

# 4. Edit components, parameters, provenance
$EDITOR data/standards/binaryPairs/NRTL/benzene-methanol.dat

# 5. Restore read-only
chmod -w data/standards/binaryPairs/NRTL/benzene-methanol.dat
chmod -w data/standards/binaryPairs/NRTL/

# 6. Commit
git add data/standards/binaryPairs/NRTL/benzene-methanol.dat
git commit -m "Add NRTL benzene-methanol from DECHEMA Vol I Part 2a, p. 234"
```
/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  Yang 2020 polymer group contributions for the GLASS TRANSITION Tg(inf).

  Source (genuinely open, redistributable -- unlike the Van Krevelen book):
    Yang, Zou, Ye, Zhu, Dong, Bi, "A Modified Group Contribution Scheme to
    Predict Homopolymer Glass Transition Temperatures", ACS Omega 5 (2020)
    19655, doi:10.1021/acsomega.0c04499 -- CC-BY 4.0.
    Values transcribed FAITHFULLY from Supporting Information Table S3
    ("Formula weights and contributions of groups used to predict Tg(inf)",
    pp. S10-S13), fetched via the europepmc open mirror (PMC7676332).

  THE EQUATION (paper eq 5):
    Tg(inf) = [ sum N_i*Yg_i(inf) + Nb*Ygb ] / M0
      Yg in 10^3 g.K/mol ; M0 = sum N_i*MW_i in g/mol  -> Tg in K.

  Nb / Ygb -- THE BACKBONE-IN-THE-SIDE-CHAIN TERM.
    `Ygb` (group `backboneSideChain`, SI row 58, Yg = 2.771) is the per-occurrence
    contribution `Nb` counts.  Nb = number of backbone atoms that sit in a SIDE
    chain (not on the main chain).  For the common vinyl polymers (PVC,
    polystyrene, poly(vinylpyridine), ...) Nb = 0, so the bracket is just
    sum N_i*Yg_i.  When a case has side-chain backbone atoms, add the group
    `backboneSideChain` with `count Nb` -- it is an ordinary additive term.

  GROUP IDENTITIES -- VERIFIED 2026-06-22.
    Table S3 draws each group as a 2D-structure IMAGE.  Rows S10-S11 (groups
    1-35) were matched to main-chain repeat-unit fragments and CROSS-VALIDATED
    against the paper's Fig 6: PVC (CH2 + CHCl) = 351 K, poly(4-vinylpyridine)
    (CH2 + CH4pyridyl) = 426 K, poly(N-vinylpyrrolidone) (CH2 + CH2pyrrolidinone)
    = 452 K, poly(N-vinylcarbazole) (CH2 + CHcarbazolyl) = 504 K -- all EXACT vs
    the paper.  Rows S12-S13 (groups 36-58) are the linker / aromatic / heteroatom
    groups, transcribed from the SI figures.

  *  Groups marked `vanKrevelen` in `provenance.note` carry an asterisk in
     Table S3 ("Group found in Van Krevelen's book"): the FW + Yg are reported by
     Yang 2020 (this is what we cite + redistribute), the structure originally
     catalogued by Van Krevelen.

  The engine REFUSES to write under data/standards/ -- editing this file is a
  CURATION act (see CLAUDE.md SS7).
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

groups
(
    // ---- Main-chain repeat-unit groups (Table S3 rows 1-35) -- VERIFIED -----
    { name CH2;                MW 14;    Yg 4.026;
      structure "-CH2-";
      provenance { origin "Yang et al., ACS Omega 5 (2020) 19655, Table S3 (CC-BY)"; method "limiting Tg group contribution Yg(inf)"; note "unambiguous SI text label; verified via PVC/4VP/NVP/NVC = 351/426/452/504 K"; } }

    { name CHCH3;              MW 28;    Yg 8.222;
      structure "-CH(CH3)-";
      provenance { origin "Yang et al., ACS Omega 5 (2020) 19655, Table S3 (CC-BY)"; note "row 2"; } }

    { name CHOCH3;             MW 44;    Yg 10.611;
      structure "-CH(OCH3)-";
      provenance { origin "Yang et al., ACS Omega 5 (2020) 19655, Table S3 (CC-BY)"; note "row 3"; } }

    { name CHCOOH;             MW 58;    Yg 18.820;
      structure "-CH(COOH)-";
      provenance { origin "Yang et al., ACS Omega 5 (2020) 19655, Table S3 (CC-BY)"; note "row 4"; } }

    { name CHOH;               MW 30;    Yg 4.697;
      structure "-CH(OH)-";
      provenance { origin "Yang et al., ACS Omega 5 (2020) 19655, Table S3 (CC-BY)"; note "row 5"; } }

    { name CHCONH2;            MW 57;    Yg 29.918;
      structure "-CH(CONH2)-";
      provenance { origin "Yang et al., ACS Omega 5 (2020) 19655, Table S3 (CC-BY)"; note "row 6"; } }

    { name CHcyclopentyl;      MW 82;    Yg 29.607;
