/*--------------------------------*- Choupo -*--------------------------------*\
  Edwards (1978) molecule-ion interaction parameter, CO2 - OH.

  Role: the molecule-ion (SALTING-OUT) beta(0) of Table 6, a quadratic in T.

  beta1 is NOT stored: for any pair involving a MOLECULAR species the paper
  sets beta1 = 0 ("because experimental data are inadequate").

  Table 6 is headed "for 0 to 170 degC" and its polynomials are written in
  T.  **T IS KELVIN.**  The paper settles this itself: Table 7's column II
  publishes beta0(NH3,NH4+) = 1/2 beta0(NH3,NH3) + 1/2 beta0(CO2,NH4+)
  - 1/2 beta0(CO2,CO2) = 0.051 at 100 degC, which evaluates to 0.0504 in
  kelvin and 0.0582 in Celsius.  The degC in the header is the range of
  validity, exactly as in Tables 1 and 3.
\*-----------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
recordType    electrolytePairParameters;
schemaVersion 1;

model         edwardsPitzer;
convention    Edwards-Hkgatm-v1;

pair { molecule CO2aq; ion OH; }

parameters
{
    //  beta0 = a0 + a1 T + a2 T^2,  T in KELVIN
    a0  +0.26;
    a1  -0.00162;
    a2  +2.89e-06;
}

Trange ( 273.15 443.15 );   // 0-170 degC

source "Edwards, Maurer, Newman & Prausnitz, AIChE J. 24(6):966-976 (1978), Table 6";
/*--------------------------------*- Choupo -*--------------------------------*\
  Edwards (1978) single-ion interaction parameter for bisulfide (HS).

  Role: the SINGLE-ION contribution beta_pm(0) of Table 5.  An ion-ion
  beta(0) is NOT stored anywhere: it is DERIVED at assembly time by
  Bromley's additivity, Eq 22,  beta(0)_+- = beta_+(0) + beta_-(0).
  Storing the sums as well would be two homes for one fact.

  The paper assumes interaction parameters between ions of LIKE SIGN are
  ZERO (Bronsted 1922/1923), and calls the whole table "preliminary results
  subject to change as more and better data become available".  A case
  using these may not present them as measured constants.

  Temperature dependence is assumed NEGLIGIBLE for the ion contributions --
  the paper says so explicitly, supported by beta(1) data for strong
  electrolytes at various temperatures.

  The SULFIDE ion S= (Table 5: 0.007) is deliberately NOT curated with this
  record: no species/S exists in the catalogue and the shipped sulfide
  network carries the FIRST dissociation only (Table 1's HS- row, K = 0.018
  Kw, is deferred with it).  Curating an ion no network reaches would be a
  record with no reader.
\*-----------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
recordType    electrolytePairParameters;
schemaVersion 1;

model         edwardsPitzer;
convention    Edwards-Hkgatm-v1;

ion HS;

parameters
{
    beta0_ion  0.074;   // kg/mol
}

source "Edwards, Maurer, Newman & Prausnitz, AIChE J. 24(6):966-976 (1978), Table 5";
/*--------------------------------*- Choupo -*--------------------------------*\
  Edwards (1978) molecule-ion interaction parameter, NH3 - H.

  Role: the molecule-ion (SALTING-OUT) beta(0) of Table 6, temperature-independent.

  beta1 is NOT stored: for any pair involving a MOLECULAR species the paper
  sets beta1 = 0 ("because experimental data are inadequate").

  Table 6 is headed "for 0 to 170 degC" and its polynomials are written in
  T.  **T IS KELVIN.**  The paper settles this itself: Table 7's column II
  publishes beta0(NH3,NH4+) = 1/2 beta0(NH3,NH3) + 1/2 beta0(CO2,NH4+)
  - 1/2 beta0(CO2,CO2) = 0.051 at 100 degC, which evaluates to 0.0504 in
  kelvin and 0.0582 in Celsius.  The degC in the header is the range of
  validity, exactly as in Tables 1 and 3.
\*-----------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
recordType    electrolytePairParameters;
schemaVersion 1;

model         edwardsPitzer;
convention    Edwards-Hkgatm-v1;

pair { molecule NH3aq; ion H; }

parameters
{
    beta0  +0.015;   // kg/mol, T-independent
}

Trange ( 273.15 443.15 );   // 0-170 degC

source "Edwards, Maurer, Newman & Prausnitz, AIChE J. 24(6):966-976 (1978), Table 6";
