  Edwards (1978) molecule-molecule interaction parameter for HYDROGEN SULFIDE.

  Role: beta0_aa of the Pitzer truncation of Eq 8, for the like pair
  (H2Saq, H2Saq).  Consumed by the `edwardsPitzer` activity engine.

  Eq 14:  beta0_aa = E + F/T      [kg/mol],  T in KELVIN.

  beta1 is NOT stored: for any pair involving a MOLECULAR species the paper
  sets beta1 = 0 ("because experimental data are inadequate").  A zero that
  the model applies by rule is not a datum, and writing it here would invite
  someone to change it.
\*-----------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
recordType    electrolytePairParameters;
schemaVersion 1;

model         edwardsPitzer;
convention    Edwards-Hkgatm-v1;

pair { molecule H2Saq; molecule2 H2Saq; }

parameters
{
    E   -0.2106;      // kg/mol
    F   61.56;        // (kg/mol) K
}

Trange ( 273.15 423.15 );   // 0-150 degC: Eq 14 is valid "for the same
                            // temperature range as that used for Henry's
                            // constants", and Table 3's H2S row says 0-150.
                            // (Corrected 2026-08-24 from 0-170, which is the
                            // FIGURE 4 span, not the correlation's -- found
                            // by the independent review against the paper.)
source "Edwards, Maurer, Newman & Prausnitz, AIChE J. 24(6):966-976 (1978), Table 4";
/*--------------------------------*- Choupo -*--------------------------------*\
  Edwards (1978) single-ion interaction parameter for ammonium (NH4).

  Role: the SINGLE-ION contribution beta_pm(0) of Table 5.  An ion-ion
  beta(0) is NOT stored anywhere: it is DERIVED at assembly time by
  Bromley's additivity, Eq 22,  beta(0)_+- = beta_+(0) + beta_-(0).
  Storing the sums as well would be two homes for one fact.

  The paper assumes interaction parameters between ions of LIKE SIGN are
  ZERO (Bronsted 1922/1923), and calls the whole table "preliminary results
  subject to change as more and better data become available".  A case
  using these may not present them as measured constants.

  Temperature dependence is assumed NEGLIGIBLE for the ion contributions --
  the paper says so explicitly, supported by beta(1) data for strong
  electrolytes at various temperatures.
\*-----------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
recordType    electrolytePairParameters;
schemaVersion 1;

model         edwardsPitzer;
convention    Edwards-Hkgatm-v1;

ion NH4;

parameters
{
    beta0_ion  -0.028;   // kg/mol
}

source "Edwards, Maurer, Newman & Prausnitz, AIChE J. 24(6):966-976 (1978), Table 5";
/*--------------------------------*- Choupo -*--------------------------------*\
  Edwards (1978) single-ion interaction parameter for hydroxide (OH).

